    "- Apply transformations such as log returns\n",
    "- Remove extreme values when justified"
   ],
   "id": "372a3b2228414eab"
  },
  {
   "metadata": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Challenge 2: Skewness",
   "id": "930643e47f480580"
  },
  {
   "metadata": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "Skewness measures the asymmetry of a probability distribution. It indicates whether the data is evenly distributed around the mean or skewed to one side.",
   "id": "181f05aaf08d03f4"
  },
  {
   "metadata": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "In financial data, skewness helps identify whether returns are more prone to extreme gains or losses.",
   "id": "f70e60a6689a69ff"
  },
  {
   "metadata": {
    "ExecuteTime": {
     "end_time": "2026-05-05T10:55:39.936492900Z",
     "start_time": "2026-05-05T10:55:39.758679900Z"
    }
   },
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": [
    "from scipy.stats import skew\n",
    "\n",
    "skew_returns = skew(data['Returns'])\n",
    "print(\"Skewness of Returns:\", skew_returns)"
   ],
   "id": "dde52708f2a674fd",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Skewness of Returns: 0.15465220802884366\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "execution_count": 47
  },
  {
   "metadata": {
